SET
  @Description_cb8e0e69 = N'Store this — it is the only handle for the Cancellation API. PK — SOFT key, Tier-1 (definitive): addressing-proof @ packages/Integration/connectors-registry/elevate/sources/elevate-report-api.derived.json#/components/schemas/RegistrationCreateResponse/properties/registration_id + required[] of RegistrationCancelRequest. WRITE-SURFACE field: proven on the registration write body only; its read-side visibility through the Report API is UNPROVEN in every source checked, so it is nullable on read. PK FALSIFIED AND SURFACE CORRECTED — this field is WRITE-SIDE ONLY. RealityProbe packages/Integration/connectors-registry/elevate/runs/connector-elevate-1787634178034-39f0b3cc/output/elevate-read-probe.mjs (amendment round 1, 2026-08-25): the read door REJECTS a request for this field on resource ''productRegistration'' — POST fields={registration_id:true} -> HTTP 500, 59B, {"error":{"message":"Field registration_id doesn''t exist"}}. This is a FALSIFIED PK, not a null one: no retrievable identity column of this name exists on the read resource. Doc re-read confirms the probe and explains the original error: vendor-article-16000139233-registration-api documents registration_id ONLY as (a) a value RETURNED by the Registration API create response ({ product_url, registration_id }) and (b) a value the caller POSTs to the Cancellation API — verbatim, "registration_id, which is the ID of the registration on this end, which can be used later if you plan to use the Cancellation API" and "This ID identifies the order in Elevate, and this will need to be stored in your system to use the Cancellation API later." It is NEVER named as a Report API read field in ANY source. Declaring it as the read PK conflated the write handle with a read column. The field is RETAINED (it is a real, doc-evidenced write-contract handle the connector must persist from the create response to make cancel possible) but marked Configuration.surface=''write-only''. CRITICAL: it MUST be excluded from the read `fields` allow-list — Elevate reads require an explicit field selector, so including it would return HTTP 500 and fail EVERY productRegistration read, not just one column. PK REINSTATED AS THE RECORD KEY, READ EXCLUSION UNCHANGED (2026-08-25). The probe above falsified exactly ONE claim — that this column is RETRIEVABLE through the Report API `fields` allow-list — and that claim stays falsified: Configuration.surface=''write-only'' and Configuration.excludeFromReadFieldSelector=true remain in force, and ElevateConnector.ReadColumnsFor drops the column on those two flags alone (never on IsPrimaryKey), so not one byte of any read selector changes. What the probe did NOT test, and therefore did not falsify, is the separate claim that this value IDENTIFIES a registration — which the vendor states outright ("This ID identifies the order in Elevate") and which the Cancellation API proves by ADDRESSING: registration_id is a required member of RegistrationCancelRequest and is the SOLE record selector that endpoint accepts. Encoding "not read-projectable" as IsPrimaryKey=false asserted the strictly stronger "this object has no record key", which the evidence does not support and which left a WRITABLE object (SupportsCreate + SupportsDelete, and the delete is keyed on THIS value) with no key for MJ to address a row by — the empty-record-identifier save failure that scripts/lint-writable-pk.mjs guards. Type widened Integer -> String in the same change and for the same reason: this column carries the record''s EXTERNAL IDENTITY from either provenance — the vendor''s integer registration_id for rows this connector CREATED, and the connector''s minted content-hash identity for rows the READ door returned (ElevateConnector.ToElevateRecord back-fills the single declared PK whenever the source cannot supply it, which for this object is always, because the door has no such column). An INT column cannot hold a 64-character sha256, so keeping Type=Integer would have traded a missing key for a conversion failure on every read-sourced save. The engine''s own runtime path already types integration identity columns this way (IntegrationSchemaSync.MapSourceType maps integer -> nvarchar, "stored as nvarchar in integration schemas"). The key stays SOFT: applySoftPKFKConfig sets the entity METADATA PK only and DDLGenerator emits NO PRIMARY KEY and NO UNIQUE constraint for it, so a wrong inference can never reject a valid row.'
SET
